From: Oliver Sargison <sarol263@student.otago.ac.nz>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:17:38 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 38/54] Plutonium: tabs' permission prompts ask the shell

EXO-TAB-PERMISSIONS (docs/features/TAB_FEATURES.md, "Permission prompts"):
a registry tab's permission requests become a tab request of kind
"permission", answered by the shell or, for deny and dismiss, an agent.

- Tab::AttachHelpers() attaches PermissionRequestManager,
  PageSpecificContentSettings and FileSystemAccessPermissionRequestManager
  (plutonium_permissions.{h,cc}). Tab's RequestMediaAccessPermission() and
  CheckMediaAccessPermission() go to MediaCaptureDevicesDispatcher, as
  BrowserWebContentsDelegate's do, so getUserMedia() works in tabs.
- CreatePermissionPrompt() returns plutonium_permissions' prompt for a
  shell's tab. Its detail is {origin, embeddingOrigin, thread, types,
  scopes}; the answer {answer: allow | deny | dismiss, scope: once |
  everywhere} becomes AcceptThisTime, Accept, Deny or Dismiss. An agent's
  "allow" is refused.
- ShowFileSystemAccessPermissionDialog() asks the same way for a shell's
  tab (types "file-write" or "file-read", with the files' names).
- Fallback (no shell took it, or it went away): dismissed, and the tab's
  state records it in "blocked": [{origin, permission}] until the page
  navigates to another document.
- PermissionRequestManager::set_prompt_while_hidden(): a tab no <embed>
  shows still asks, so its shell and agents see pending.permission.
- The tab's state has "inUse": {camera, microphone, screen} while it
  captures (MediaStreamCaptureIndicator).
